import{_ as s,o as i,c as a,an as t}from"./chunks/framework.CGmhhfZ6.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Patterns to Avoid","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"perf/patterns_to_avoid.md","filePath":"perf/patterns_to_avoid.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"perf/patterns_to_avoid.md"};function l(o,e,r,h,d,p){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="Patterns-to-Avoid" tabindex="-1">Patterns to Avoid <a class="header-anchor" href="#Patterns-to-Avoid" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Patterns to Avoid {#Patterns-to-Avoid}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>By default, behaviors that hinder performance, like scalar indexing, converting a 0D NDArray to a Julia scalar, and implicit type promotion, are disabled. The <code>@allow</code> macros permit users to selectively opt in to these behaviors despite the potential performance loss. They are useful for inspecting values while debugging, checking small test cases, or integrating external packages whose algorithms need host-side decisions or type conversions.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Macro</th><th style="text-align:left;">What it permits</th><th style="text-align:left;">Why it is guarded</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>@allowscalar</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Accessing individual array elements, including in <code>for</code> loops</td><td style="text-align:left;">Repeated device-to-host transfers and possible communication across nodes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>@allowfetch</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Implicitly extracting device scalars for comparisons, predicates, or conversion to Julia numbers</td><td style="text-align:left;">The host must wait for the result</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>@allowpromotion</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Implicit conversion of arrays to wider element types</td><td style="text-align:left;">Extra copies, memory use, and potentially slower arithmetic</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Wrap the smallest expression or block that needs the permission. Macro and <code>do</code>-block forms restore the previous setting when the scope exits, including on errors. Each permission is independent: <code>@allowfetch</code> does not enable scalar indexing or wider type promotion.</p><p>For external packages, enable only the permissions required by the code path being used. The <a href="./../examples/krylov">Krylov.jl example</a> uses <code>@allowfetch</code> for convergence decisions and also <code>@allowpromotion</code> for complex CG. <a href="./../examples/ordinarydiffeq">OrdinaryDiffEq.jl</a> can likewise encounter host-side checks or generic array operations, depending on the solver and options. The documented heat example uses settings that need no <code>@allow</code> macros. Enabling a permission does not implement missing methods or establish that an untested solver is compatible. Check correctness and performance for your configuration.</p><h2 id="Scalar-indexing" tabindex="-1">Scalar indexing <a class="header-anchor" href="#Scalar-indexing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scalar indexing {#Scalar-indexing}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Accessing an <code>NDArray</code> one element at a time, such as <code>A[5]</code>, requires a host value and can be expensive, especially in a loop. Prefer broadcasting, reductions, and array operations. To inspect an element while debugging:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">using</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> cuNumeric</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> cuNumeric</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">rand</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(Float32, </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">16</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">value </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> @allowscalar</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> A[</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">5</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The equivalent scoped form is <code>allowscalar() do ... end</code>. Avoid enabling scalar indexing for an entire program: it can hide slow element-by-element fallbacks in otherwise generic Julia code.</p><h2 id="Fetching-device-scalars" tabindex="-1">Fetching device scalars <a class="header-anchor" href="#Fetching-device-scalars" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Fetching device scalars {#Fetching-device-scalars}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Reductions such as <code>sum(A)</code> return a <code>CNScalar</code> that stays in runtime-managed storage. Keep it there when using it in further arithmetic. Call <code>fetch</code> when you deliberately need a native Julia number. It waits for the result and needs neither <code>@allowfetch</code> nor <code>@allowscalar</code>.</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">s </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> sum</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(A)                  </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># device scalar</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">scaled </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.*</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> s             </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># use it without fetching</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">host_sum </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> fetch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(s)          </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># explicit host retrieval</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Use <code>@allowfetch</code> when code implicitly needs a host value, such as a solver comparing a residual with its stopping tolerance:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">above_threshold </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> @allowfetch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> s </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1f0</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">host_sum </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> allowfetch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">() </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">do</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">    Float32</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(s)</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">end</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Arithmetic inside the scope still runs on the backend. Values are fetched only where host extraction is required. Repeated comparisons can force synchronization, so keep them out of inner loops when array operations can express the same calculation. <code>@allowfetch</code> does not automatically convert arguments for arbitrary methods: a function requiring a <code>Float32</code> still needs an explicit <code>Float32(s)</code> conversion.</p><p>See <a href="./../api_cnscalar">Device Scalars</a> and the <a href="/cuNumeric.jl/previews/PR250/examples/tensoroperations#Scalars">TensorOperations.jl scalar example</a>.</p><h2 id="Implicit-promotion" tabindex="-1">Implicit promotion <a class="header-anchor" href="#Implicit-promotion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Implicit promotion {#Implicit-promotion}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Mixing different element types can widen an array, creating a copy and increasing memory use. A common cause is mixing a <code>Float32</code> array with a <code>Float64</code> literal: Julia&#39;s <code>1.0</code> is <code>Float64</code>, whereas <code>1f0</code> is <code>Float32</code>. Match constants to the array&#39;s element type when wider precision is not needed:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">B </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.+</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1f0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                # keep Float32 arithmetic</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">C </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> @allowpromotion</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> A </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">.+</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1.0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"> # deliberately allow widening to Float64</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Use <code>@allowpromotion</code> (or <code>allowpromotion() do ... end</code>) when the conversion is intentional, including where an external algorithm mixes coefficient types. The macro permits the conversion. It does not remove its allocation or performance cost.</p>`,20)])])}const g=s(n,[["render",l]]);export{k as __pageData,g as default};
